'''Ge{{ayin}}ez''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|ɡ|iː|ɛ|z}}<ref>{{multiref| Laurie Bauer, 2007, ''The Linguistics Student's Handbook'', Edinburgh | {{OED|Geez}} }}</ref> or {{IPAc-en|ɡ|iː|ˈ|ɛ|z}};<ref>{{multiref| {{MW|Geez}} | {{dict.com|Geez}} }}</ref> {{lang|gez|ግዕዝ}} {{transliteration|sem|Gə{{ayin}}(ə)z}}<ref>{{multiref| {{harvnb|Lambdin|1978|p=400}}: Ge‘z | {{harvnb|Leslau|1989|p=209}}: gə{{ayin}}əz | {{harvnb|Leslau|1987|p=175}}: gəʿz | {{harvnb|Cohen|1921|p=217|loc={{lang|fr|«il vaut mieux préciser en <em>éthiopien classique</em> ou employer le nom indigène; celui-ci est {{lang|gez|ግእዝ፡}}, c’est-à-dire en prononciation restituée {{tlit|sem|gə‘əz}} ou {{tlit|sem|gə‘z}}, et {{tlit|sem|gəəz}} dans la prononciation abyssine actuelle»}}{{pb}}(it is worth it to be precise using ''Classical Ethiopic'' or the indigenous name, which is {{lang|gez|ግእዝ፡}}, that is, in reconstructed pronunciation, {{tlit|sem|gə‘əz}} or {{tlit|sem|gə‘z}}, and {{tlit|sem|gəəz}} [i.e. IPA {{IPA|lang=gez|[ˈgɨʔɨz]}} with a glottal stop] in today's Abyssinian pronunciation) }} }}</ref> {{IPA|gez|ˈɡɨʕ(ɨ)z||Ge'ez.ogg}}, and sometimes referred to in scholarly literature as '''Classical Ethiopic''') is an ancient [[South Semitic languages|South Semitic language]]. The language originates from what is now [[Ethiopia]] and [[Eritrea]].

As of today, Ge{{ayin}}ez is used as the main [[liturgical language]] of the [[Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church]], the [[Eritrean Orthodox Tewahedo Church]], the [[Ethiopian Catholic Church]], the [[Eritrean Catholic Church]], and the [[Beta Israel]] [[Jews|Jewish]] community.

[[Hawulti (monument)|Hawulti Obelisk]] is an ancient pre-[[Aksumite]] obelisk located in [[Matara, Eritrea|Matara]], Eritrea. The monument dates to the early Aksumite period and bears an example of the ancient Ge{{ayin}}ez script.

In one study, [[Tigre language|Tigre]] was found to have a 71% lexical similarity to Geʽez, while [[Tigrinya language|Tigrinya]] had a 68% lexical similarity to Geʽez, followed by [[Amharic]] at 62%.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Bender |first1=M. L. |title=The Languages of Ethiopia: A New Lexicostatistic Classification and Some Problems of Diffusion |journal=Anthropological Linguistics |date=May 1971 |volume=13 |issue=5 |page=173 |jstor=30029540}}</ref> Most linguists believe that Ge{{ayin}}ez does not constitute a common ancestor of modern [[Ethio-Semitic languages]] but became a separate language early on from another hypothetical unattested common language.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Connell |first1=Dan |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=SYsgpIc3mrsC&pg=PA508 |title=Historical Dictionary of Eritrea |last2=Killion |first2=Tom |publisher=Scarecrow  |year=2010 |isbn=978-0-8108-7505-0 |edition=2nd |page=508}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Haarmann |first=Harald |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=astTBgYBTOQC&pg=PA76 |title=Lexikon der untergegangenen Sprachen |publisher=C. H. Beck |year=2002 |isbn=978-3-406-47596-2 |edition=2nd |page=76 |language=de |trans-title=Lexicon of extinct languages}}</ref><ref>Amsalu Aklilu, Kuraz Publishing Agency, {{lang|gez|ጥሩ የአማርኛ ድርሰት እንዴት ያለ ነው!}} p. 42</ref>

